Red-Balloon.
If I said I have a letter for you, Would you read it?

I mean, when was the last time you read an actual letter? Not a note or a birthday card.
but an actual letter. Not those letters to tell you something, but for you to remind you that you are something.

You know, there were whole generations that relied on these letters to hear from their loved ones. It wasn’t made out of just paper. But of hope that it should go to the rightful owner at the right time. I mean, we don’t even get replies after weeks. (Now, don’t look at me like that. It’s just everyone is busy, and I am lazy.)
That letter could have carried someone’s news of joy, fear, tears or even love.
(and there was no assurance that it made it in time.)

The best thing that writing letters gave me is a chance to speak my heart out.
Writing letters isn't something that comes so easily for me, but I still try.
I generally take birthdays as an occasion to remind someone who they are.
And when they tell me how it puts a smile on their face, it truly is an amazing feeling.
I personally don’t like mine. And I was scared that maybe one might forget themselves and feel the same. (I said I don’t like mine. I can’t wait till they hate theirs too.)
So, I write and let them know what a blessing they truly are. like they deserve to.
(I don’t know what you are gifting someone. A letter just makes it more personal. So, just don’t overthink this, just write that damn six-page letter to them. It’s not like it’s their farewell every day.)

I thought love letters were the worst. What do you mean here, I am confessing to the love of my life, and there is gonna be a grammatical error forever in that letter? (not that he will accept it if there wasn’t an error.) The whole point is that I wrote it down, so I can’t mess it up. (She’s lying, guys. She was just too scared to talk to him.)

Anyway, he is gone. But I am still obsessed with the idea of writing these letters.
(What do you mean I have to put all this under a caption in my Instagram story?)
I have so much to tell and can’t let it just die in a dust-collecting diary that I forget exists until someone hurts me, and I have no one to cry?
(She needs it every day. But, again. Just too lazy)
And now, I write.


This isn’t my first time writing letters to someone.
But sure, it's the first time to write one, without knowing who, when and how it might reach. Like I left my red balloon in the blue sky. And I hope it found someone and made them smile.
And I will never know about it.
 
But, sitting here, I can simply hope that it finds someone who needed it the most.
There might be someone who saw it out their window and felt happy for how free it was in the blue sky. Maybe it reminded someone that they are still kids and deserve a beautiful balloon. Maybe someone liked the pop of red in the plain blue sky.
Or maybe it simply have made someone happy.
like a smile. like the first of summer breeze, like a warm hug.
(and if this made you think you should get a balloon for yourself, you should do so right away.)

I also write with the intention that birthdays shouldn’t be the only day that one is celebrated. You, in your worst moments, finding courage to look at the bright side and choose love like the one happy for the red balloon flying past his window, the red balloon that he never even saw before, the red balloon he might never even see again.
 
I don’t know how life is treating you, but without you, it’s just a lost balloon. 
And even with a heavy heart, you choosing love is what makes me write this letter.
I write this letter to celebrate you, and when it’s your turn, let it go.
 There might be someone looking out their window for a sign.
But for now, I just can’t wait for this red balloon to find you.

And again, it all comes down to the question,
If I said there was a red balloon in the sky, would you take a moment to cherish it?
If I said I have a letter for you, Would you read it?

Love,
The Little Girl.
